# HDD Cage Fan Modifications

---

A collection of instructions for improving the cooling on various drive cages.
I tend to use [Noctua][] fans, as they work well for reducing temperatures
while remaining fairly quite. My home server is in a closet next to my 
kitchen / living room, so reducing both the temperature and noise is crucial.

[Noctua]: https://www.noctua.at/

\

## IStar BPN-DE350HD

---

:::: side-by-side
:::

This mod is based on [iStarUSA BPN-DE350SS Mod][] by Tybio on the unraid forums,
which in turn is based on [IStar Hard Drive Cage Fan Replacement Mod][] by
Henness. They describe the step by step process for replacing the cheap 80mm
fans with better Noctua 80mm fans.

[IStar Hard Drive Cage Fan Replacement Mod]: https://www.instructables.com/IStar-Hard-Drive-Cage-Fan-Replacement-Mod/
[iStarUSA BPN-DE350SS Mod]: https://forums.unraid.net/topic/71462-istarusa-bpn-de350ss-mod/

You will need:

- [ ] Screwdriver
- [ ] Flush cutters
- [ ] Drill with 7/64" Drill bit
- [ ] Weather Striping
- [ ] 4x 6-32 x 1-1/4" Screws / Nuts
- [ ] 80mm Replacement Fan (I'm using a Noctua NF-A8)

:::
{style="width: 20rem;"}
![IStar Front](/images/fans/istar_bpn_de350hd_front.png)
::::

### Step 1: Remove the Old Fan and Shroud

Remove the old fan and shroud by unscrewing the four screws on each
corner and unplugging the 3-pin JST connector. You can discard the cheap fan and
shroud as these are no longer needed.

{style="width: 20rem;"}
![IStar Step 1](/images/fans/istar_bpn_de350hd_step1.png)

### Step 2: Drill Out the Fan Mounting Holes

Cut the fan alignment dowels (tips of the mounting holes) off the four mounting
holes using a pair of flush cutters. Using a 7/64" drill bit, drill out the
four mounting holes, to make them wider. Be careful not to hit the PCB on the
other side with the drill bit.

{style="width: 20rem;"}
![IStar Step 2](/images/fans/istar_bpn_de350hd_step2.png)

### Step 3: Cut and Attach the Weather Strips

Cut Weather stripping to form a "Box" around the fan stand-offs. Don't worry
about covering the 3-pin connection or the fan selector switch as you will not
use either again.

{style="width: 20rem;"}
![IStar Step 3](/images/fans/istar_bpn_de350hd_step3.png)

### Step 4: Screw Your New Fan On

Take four 6-32 x 1-1/4" screws, and spin one nut up the length of each screw to
act as a spacer. Then install the new fan. Take care not to strip the new
plastic mounting holes (a screwdriver is recommended). Attach the new fan
connector directly to one of your motherboard fan headers or a fan hub.

{style="width: 20rem;"}
![IStar Step 4](/images/fans/istar_bpn_de350hd_step4.png)

\

## Norco SS-500

---

:::: side-by-side
:::

The Norco is significantly easier to work with, as the fan can just be replaced
without any modifications.

You will need:

- [ ] Screwdriver
- [ ] 80mm Replacement Fan (I'm using a Noctua NF-A8)

:::
{style="width: 20rem;"}
![Norco Front](/images/fans/norco_ss500_front.png)
::::

### Step 1: Remove the Old Fan and Shroud

Remove the four screws on the back holding the fan shroud on. Then remove the
four screws holding the old fan to the shroud. Discard the old fan, but keep
the shroud, as we will reuse it.

{style="width: 40rem;"}
![Norco Step 1](/images/fans/norco_ss500_step1.png)

### Step 2: Install the New Fan

Screw the new fan to the shroud, then connect the fan header to the 4-pin fan
header on the back of the Norco. Reattach the shroud, and screw it back into
place.

::: side-by-side
{style="max-width: 20rem;"}
![Norco Step 2](/images/fans/norco_ss500_step2.png)

{style="max-width: 40rem;"}
![Norco Installed](/images/fans/norco_ss500_step_final.png)
:::

\

## ICY DOCK MB602SPO-B

---

:::: side-by-side
:::

The ICY dock requires a small modification to the metal chassis to allow the 4-pin fan connector to fit


You will need:

- [ ] Screwdriver
- [ ] Pliers (alternately a tool to bend/cut metal)
- [ ] 40x20mm Replacement Fan (I'm using a Noctua NF-A4x20)

:::
{style="width: 20rem;"}
![ICY Front](/images/fans/icy_dock_front.png)
::::

### Step 1: Remove the Old Fan and Back Plate

Remove the two screws on the back holding the fan to the back plate. Then
remove the screws holding the backplate on. Discard the old fan.

{style="width: 40rem;"}
![ICY Step 1](/images/fans/icy_dock_step1.png)

### Step 2: Modify the Chassis (4-pin only)

To make room for a four pin connector the chassis must be cut. Remove the metal
T shape around the connectors to the right of the fan. I bent and snapped them
off with pliers, but you may have better tools.

::: side-by-side
{style="max-width: 20rem;"}
![ICY Step 2A](/images/fans/icy_dock_step2a.png)

{style="max-width: 20rem;"}
![ICY Step 2B](/images/fans/icy_dock_step2b.png)
:::

### Step 3: Install the New Fan

Reattach the back plate, then screw the new fan onto it with the two fan screws.
Connect the fan header to the 4-pin fan header on the back of the ICY dock.

{style="width: 40rem;"}
![ICY Step 3](/images/fans/icy_dock_step3.png)
